<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- NuGet convention for F# analyzers: FSAC/CLI load them from
         analyzers/dotnet/fs. IncludeBuildOutput=false + the explicit None
         packs below keep the dlls FLAT in that folder (BuildOutputTargetFolder
         alone nests them under net8.0/, one directory below the path every
         setup guide tells users to configure). -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <PackageId>FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers</PackageId>
    <Description>Functional refactoring suggestions (light bulb quick fixes) for F#, built on FSharp.Analyzers.SDK. Over 130 rules covering correctness, performance, idiom and redundancy; every one is Hint severity, so they never gate a build.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.8.23: A code audit of the whole source and of everything since 0.8.12, each finding reproduced through the rule before being fixed. Rewrites that compiled and silently changed behaviour now stand down. FR0157 keeps a match with a guarded catch-all (`| v when v.Length = 4 ->` above `| "POST" ->` was deleted as dead), a `| null ->` arm, or a variable pattern two module-level constants spell, and resolves a constant by its declaration rather than its name (a nested module's `let kind = "dir"` was read as the file's `let kind = "file"`). FR0156 refuses a loop whose other statements are not unit (a discarded `d.TryAdd(x, x)` had become a second yield), a delegate element type (Add converted the lambda where a yield does not) and a Span read in the moved region; FR0158 asks the typed tree the same Span question and stands down without one. FR0071 hoists only arithmetic, comparison and boolean operators over values no call in the loop can change (`let line = reader |> readLine` had left the loop reading one line forever). FR0050 spells `sum`/`sumBy` for a floating accumulator only, since FSharp.Core's sum is checked where the loop wrapped; an integer accumulation gets the `fold`. FR0107 requires a predicate of FSharp.Core and string members only, so a logging `validate f` runs as often as before. FR0004 keeps the eager copy in front of a lambda that mutates a collection or names the source (`rs |> Seq.toList |> List.iter (fun x -> rs.Remove x |> ignore)` threw once it enumerated `rs` itself). FR0003 composes only stages whose arguments are literals or immutable values (`addN (compute())` had run once instead of per element). FR0044 turns into `reraise ()` only a binder of the whole exception, never a case field (`ParseFailed(_, inner) -> raise inner` had rethrown the wrapper). FR0118 leaves the calls of a `with` handler or `finally` alone, where a cancelled operation must still roll back. FR0075 treats a task the disposable produced and handed to any call as in flight (`tasks.Add(client.GetStringAsync u)` then `Task.WhenAll` disposed the client under its requests). FR0015 spells the ordinal `StartsWith` overload and keeps a regex ending in `$`, which matches before a final newline. FR0039 keeps OrdinalIgnoreCase as the fix for every lowering (the current-culture `ToLower()` differs from it on the Turkish dotless i alone, and the idiomatic spelling is taken over a CurrentCultureIgnoreCase nobody writes), and a lowered call that already names a StringComparison keeps the author's choice: `x.ToLower().StartsWith("a", StringComparison.Ordinal)` becomes `x.StartsWith("a", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)`, where the tuple argument had stood the rule down. Guards that had closed too much reopened to their precise condition: FR0107 follows a same-file predicate into its body and rewrites when nothing there has an effect, standing down only for a callee it cannot read; FR0071 hoists `/` and `%` over a non-zero literal divisor again and vetoes a read of a mutable only when a function the loop calls (same file, followed three deep) assigns it - a local `let mutable` included, since a closure has captured one as a ref cell since F# 4.0 - scanning everything between the insertion point and the loop, the pipeline head included, and treating a function-typed field, a property getter or an active pattern as a callee it cannot follow; FR0157 deletes an unguarded `| null ->` arm as the dead arm the proof shows it to be, though never one that shares an or-pattern with a literal; a built-in FR0012 hint without a typed tree stands down inside a computation-expression body only, where a custom operation can live, and fires everywhere else as before; FR0075 treats a CancellationTokenSource whose token reaches anything but an awaited BCL call - a user call, Async.Start, a constructor, a record field, a property set, a collection - as in flight; FR0029 keeps a `let` whose right side can throw (a property read such as `opt.Value` or `t.Result` counts; a record field or a plain value does not) inside the task when the task can leave the file through a public or interface member - written in one, or in a private or class binding a public member hands out - whose callers may rely on a faulted Task (SQLProvider's ISqlProvider.ExecuteSprocCommandAsync), and still hoists it from bindings that stay private. The apply tool judges a verification build "not verified" only when it stopped at the time cap (a marker on the stopped process's line, not prose), so a pre-existing MSB3073 or NU5xxx failure identical with and without the fixes keeps them as before; an abandoned typecheck is cancelled rather than left running; and a csproj or vbproj in the same solution that references the project is built as part of verification, its compiler errors weighed like the F# build's (FSharp.Azure.Quantum's C# builder cast to a union case class the run had made a struct, and the F# build alone saw success), or, where it does not build here, holds the project's public surface in place. FR0110 spells the raising arm it adds inside a computation expression with the siblings' `return` (or `yield`): a bare `raise` arm there is a unit-typed statement, and welendus's `match!` in a task had the fix rolled back on TaskCode&lt;_, unit&gt; against TaskCode&lt;_, T&gt;. FR0055 offers its IO-only narrowing of a catch-all only when the typed tree agrees that every call on the line is System.IO's (or FSharp.Core's): `Some (Path.GetFileName d, Checkpoint.loadMetadata p)` was narrowed on the strength of the `Path`, and the user function's JsonException escaped a command meant to skip junk directories (Fuuga); the assembly-loading and font-lookup probes put back by hand in SQLProvider and Kasino were the same shape. FR0142 leaves a test whose last expression is a value (NUnit's ExpectedResult was no longer compared). FR0002 keeps a match whose arm calls the enclosing `let rec` (a tail call inside `Option.map` is none). The map-fusion hints (`List.map f (List.map g x) ===> List.map (g >> f) x` and the Array and Seq twins, FSharpLint's defaults) fuse only two mappers whose calls are provably effect-free - FSharp.Core functions, union cases, and lambdas that call nothing but FSharp.Core and System.String and hold no statement, assignment, loop or printf - since the fused form interleaves calls the two sweeps ran one after the other; and a built-in hint stands down where the typed tree is not clean, so a file with an error, or a --parse-only run, no longer loses a computation expression's custom operation to `id x ===> x`. Three rewrites that did not compile: FR0049's taskify puts `return` before a parenthesised tail rather than inside it, FR0013 keeps the parentheses of an application that is itself indexed or applied (`List.sort(xs)[0]`), and `Text.patNames` reads record, cons, optional and named-field patterns, so FR0095 no longer takes an `id` a pattern bound for FSharp.Core's; FR0147 checks the bare names of patterns against an open it adds, and FR0155 treats a flexible `#Node` use as a veto. The shared byref-like guard recorded nothing for a whole file once a `ReadOnlySpan` indexer threw on its declaration location, disarming the guard for seven rules; each use now keeps its own answer. The apply tool: a rule that throws is reported and skipped, not the run ended, and counted in the exit code; a verification build that ran into its time cap, or failed without one compiler error, puts the fixes back as unverified instead of keeping them as pre-existing breakage; a typecheck abandoned after a pass wrote its files writes them back first, and the abandonment now returns at the cap (FSharp.Core's RunSynchronously waited for the check to finish regardless); the framework flags reset on every path out of the framework loop; a checkout's private SDK leaves DOTNET_ROOT and PATH when the next checkout has none; one unreadable directory no longer empties the script call-site probe. Configuration: `"Hints": false` at the root disables FR0012 as the documentation says, and the strings "true"/"false"/"on"/"off" count as booleans. The Visual Studio extension no longer writes to a sidecar that has exited (which took Visual Studio down from a timer thread), pins a fix's edits to the snapshot they were computed against and skips them when that text has moved, and keeps a failing fetch or edit off the UI thread's exception path.
 0.8.22: FR0157's serializer guard reads the VALUE handed to a reflective head, not only a type argument: a record, or a collection of one, passed or piped to `JsonSerializer.Serialize`, behind a field or property (`Serialize wrapper.Items`) or anywhere along a curried call (`Serialize options value`), stands the rule down, since every field of it is read by reflection and a union field would serialize differently. The union a run adds carries the widest visibility of the slots it types - private when they are all private, internal when one is internal or the component spans files, public only when a public one is among them - so a run without --api-changes never adds a public type to a library by itself. Two audit tests that assumed Windows path semantics now pass on Linux too.
 0.8.21: Three defects from the 0.8.20 sweep over thirty-five repositories. The api pass no longer puts cross-project edits back on a false alarm: FCS cannot hand a sibling in-memory assembly data for a project whose typecheck creates generated provided types (a JsonProvider sample), so the sibling silently bound to the dll on disk, built before the edits, and every migrated call site failed against the old signature (CarmelNet's tests); a failed in-memory check is now confirmed by a real build of the sibling before anything is put back, and the referenced project is rebuilt so the next pass reads the sibling against the edits. FR0156 keeps a pair of parentheses where the drain's were an application's own - `Some(List.ofSeq acc)` had become `Someacc`. FR0157 never gives two unions in one file the same name: two records with a `domain` field each got a `Domain`; a field's second choice now carries its record's name (`JobKind`) before the `Kind` suffix.
 0.8.20: Three idiom rules. FR0158 (new) turns a while loop that walks a mutable index while a condition holds - `let mutable line = 0` then `while line &lt; max &amp;&amp; blank line do line &lt;- line + 1`, or the countdown - into a tail-recursive local function (`advanceLine` / `retreatLine`) and an immutable `let`; measured level with the loop, where the Seq.tryFind spelling doubled the time and allocated per call. FR0156 (new) turns a ResizeArray that loops fill one Add at a time and the rest of the scope only reads into a list expression: the loops (for and while alike) move inside the brackets as they are - guards, matches, nested loops, lets and the statements around each Add included - and each Add becomes the implicit yield of its argument; List.ofSeq acc becomes acc, and Seq functions, for x in acc and an upcast to seq keep working. Measured in PerfClaims (.NET 10, 1000 ints): 20 percent faster on 30 percent less allocation than the fill-then-copy. A drain that wants an array (an index, Count, ToArray(), Array.ofSeq) stands the rule down, since the array expression measured 1.6x the ResizeArray fill's time and ResizeArray plus ToArray() is the fastest spelling of an array built one element at a time; { "FR0156": { "arrays": true } } buys the shape at that price. The declared element type becomes the list's annotation (Add converted an int literal to int64 through the method call where a bare yield would not), and an element type with subtypes to upcast from (an interface, obj, an unsealed class) stands the rule down. FR0157 (new) turns a closed set of string literals matched by name into a [&lt;RequireQualifiedAccess&gt;] union with a ToString returning the original text. The proof is a flow analysis over the string slots the value passes through - a parameter, a let, a record field, a function's return, a name a pattern binds, plain or option-wrapped - closed when every source is a literal (or a constant, [&lt;Literal&gt;] or not) or another slot and every sink a match, a comparison with a literal, a flow into a slot or a print through ToString; proven from either end, the call sites or a producing function whose every exit is a literal. Every literal source, arm and comparison becomes its case, every annotated slot is retyped, and a catch-all the proof leaves dead is dropped - it stays where the proof found a literal no arm names. A variable pattern spelling a module-level constant's name beside later arms (| us -&gt; under let us = "...", which binds a fresh us and matches everything) is taken for the comparison the author meant, and the message says so. A Result&lt;_, string&gt; is a slot like the others: its Error "..." exits are sources, its Error "..." arms the consumer, the Ok side is not followed. Under --api-changes the apply tool's api pass runs the rule with every referencing project's call sites in its world, so an exported function's literal callers count; where the host still cannot see every caller, the exported function keeps its public signature behind an adapter - a private twin typed with the union, the compilation's own callers moved to it, a wrapper of the old name mapping at the edge (OfString, whose unknown arm is the match's own raising catch-all; Result.mapError string) under a TODO. A %s hole becomes %O and a concatenation operand `string x`; a sentence-shaped literal keeps its words behind double backticks; a record a serializer fills ([&lt;CLIMutable&gt;], a Json or DataContract attribute, the type as a type argument anywhere), a dynamic message, a method call on the string, a function used as a value, a lambda parameter, and a set no producer feeds stand the rule down. The apply tool reads the whole compilation's symbol uses once, indexed by declaration and by position, so the analysis costs a few seconds per project rather than a walk per question. FR0154 is a correctness rule now and rewrites only the miss arm: TryGetValue then a store on a ConcurrentDictionary keeps its match and hit arm, and the miss arm becomes GetOrAdd(key, fun _ -&gt; compute ()), whose add is atomic. Measured at tier 1 the hit path - the one a cache takes almost every time - is 2.1 ns and nothing on TryGetValue against 7.3 ns and a 64 B delegate on GetOrAdd, so the whole-match rewrite of 0.8.12 was a slowdown sold as a speedup; the race it closes is the reason, and this shape closes it at no cost. Where running the factory ONCE matters, GetOrAdd alone does not buy it (concurrent misses each run it), so a factory that calls something carries the hint that a Lazy value - GetOrAdd(key, fun _ -&gt; lazy (compute ())).Force() - runs it once, and a Task, ValueTask or Async value, where the discarded result is a computation already running, gets that note without a fix.
 0.8.19: The build configuration the analysis does not see is no longer a blind spot. A project whose sources branch on `#if DEBUG` / `!DEBUG` / `RELEASE` / `TRACE` gets a second pass under the other configuration, where those branches are the parse tree, and its verification builds both configurations; a referencing project edited by a call-site migration is built in the other configuration too, and the migration is put back when that build fails. A call-site migration (FR0090, FR0091) keeps back a function named inside a configuration region, whose other branch has calls no parse tree holds, and one a string literal names anywhere in the project or its referencing projects - a code generator's template writes calls no symbol table lists (SQLProvider.Fable's CodeGen kept emitting the old parameter order after FR0091 reordered Row.text). The apply tool withholds any multi-line fix whose span covers a #if, #else or #endif line, whatever rule offered it.
 0.8.18: A sweep spends far less of its time in MSBuild and FCS. The compiler-argument query no longer cleans the project (a design-time build with the target forced, in place of the Rebuild that cleaned the outputs and cost a third build to put them back), and the FS3511 harvest that made every compilation recompile is cached per source tree under the local application data, so an unchanged project builds incrementally in a second or two - 28 seconds of fsc per compilation on FunStripe.Core gone. A multi-targeted project builds every framework in one parallel outer build on its narrowest pass, and the next framework's compiler arguments and typecheck run ahead on a checker of its own while the narrower pass sweeps: FCS keeps one incremental builder per project file name, so the frameworks were throwing each other's typecheck away at every switch and could never share one; the second framework's check now costs milliseconds. A file whose only conditional directives are INTERACTIVE or COMPILED - the idiom for a source that also serves as a #load'ed script - is swept once across frameworks like a directive-free file, since neither symbol varies between a project's frameworks. A script's sweep leaves the #load'ed sources a project compiles to that project: Owin.Compression's Script.fsx, typechecked as .NET Core, had written Convert.ToHexString and File.ReadAllBytesAsync into the net48 project's CompressionModule.fs. A proposed FR0155 ([&lt;Sealed&gt;] on internal classes) is benchmarked in PerfClaims before being written: the covariant array store halves and the type test does better, the virtual call does not move.
 Earlier versions: CHANGELOG.md in the repository (https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md).</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <Authors>Tuomas Hietanen</Authors>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>fsharp;analyzers;refactoring;codefix;ionide;fsautocomplete</PackageTags>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Unlicense</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <!-- analyzers are build-time tools: nothing flows to the consumer's output -->
    <DevelopmentDependency>true</DevelopmentDependency>
    <!-- analyzer packages intentionally have no lib/ folder -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <!-- the same analyzers built against FSharp.Analyzers.SDK 0.35.0 — the
         version stock Ionide's FsAutoComplete bundles. The SDK loads only
         analyzers built against its own version and skips the rest with a
         log line, so shipping both assemblies side by side lets every host
         pick the one it can use: stock Ionide loads the .Ionide.dll, the
         CLI and newer FsAutoComplete load the main one. -->
    <None
      Include="..\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide\bin\$(Configuration)\net8.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide.dll"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
    <None
      Include="bin\$(Configuration)\net8.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.dll"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
    <None
      Include="bin\$(Configuration)\net8.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.xml"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- build ordering only: the packed .Ionide.dll must exist -->
    <ProjectReference
      Include="..\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.Ionide.fsproj"
      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
      PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

